course: dictionary
lesson: 15227
title: Sewerage: the pipes under our streets
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sewerage is a noun, and it is a word we never count: you cannot say "three sewerages". It names a whole system of pipes and drains under the ground, plus the work that keeps them working. «The town is building a new sewerage system to keep the streets clean.» The clue is the word system: sewerage is the big network, not one single pipe.
explain: Read the words around it. «Clean water comes into our homes, and dirty water leaves through the sewerage.» Dirty water goes away from the house, so sewerage must be the way waste is carried off. Try your meaning in the sentence: "dirty water leaves through the underground pipes" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Sometimes the word means the work too. «The workers who do the sewerage were out all night mending a broken pipe.» Here it means the job of dealing with waste. Sewerage can also mean the waste itself, but people more often use the word sewage for that.
fact: Sewerage means the underground pipes and drains that carry dirty water and toilet waste away from homes and buildings.
fact: Sewerage is an uncountable noun: we say "some sewerage", not "three sewerages".
fact: Towns stay healthy when clean water comes in and dirty water goes out through sewers to treatment works.
fact: In 1858 London had the Great Stink, and Joseph Bazalgette built new sewers that helped stop cholera.
fact: Phrases you may see are «a sewerage system», «sewerage charges» and «water and sewerage».
exercise: «The new sewerage system runs under every street in the town.» What is a sewerage system? | A machine that washes cars | A kind of bus route | A park with a pond in it | *Underground pipes that carry dirty water away
exercise: Mum says, «The sewerage charge is on the bill.» What is the sewerage charge paying for? | Lighting the street lamps | Planting trees in the park | Buying new bicycles for the school | *Taking away and dealing with dirty water and waste
exercise: «The workers spent the night on the sewerage, fixing a broken pipe.» In this sentence, what does sewerage mean? | *The job of dealing with waste | A holiday by the sea | A bag of old toys | A type of fish
test: «Clean water comes into our homes, and dirty water leaves through the sewerage.» Where does the dirty water go? | Up into the sky as rain | Into the garden to help the flowers grow | Into the swimming pool | *Away through the underground pipes and drains
test: «Joseph Bazalgette built new sewers in London after the Great Stink of 1858.» What did his sewers help to stop? | Snow in the winter | Bee stings in the park | *Cholera | Traffic jams on the bridges
exam: Ella says, «We have three sewerages on our street.» Is Ella right? | Yes, sewerage is countable, like pipes | No, because sewerage only means clean water | *No, sewerage is uncountable, so we say some sewerage | Yes, but only in London
